{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -L -s http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/pato.owl | rg -n 'PATO_0010007|PATO_0002254|cuboidal|columnar'\"","aggregated_output":"19105:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n19108:        <rdfs:label>columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell</rdfs:label>\n19113:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n19609:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal. In mammals, endothelial cell has vimentin filaments and is derived from the mesoderm.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n19616:        <rdfs:comment>From FMA: 9.07.2001: Endothelial cell has always been classified as a kind of epithelial cell, specifically a squamous cell but that is not true. First, endothelial cell can either be squamous or cuboidal (e.g. high-endothelial cell) and secondly, it has different embryological derivation (mesodermal) than a true epithelial cell (ectodermal and endodermal). The basis for present classification is the fact that it comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures (location-based) but a better structural basis for the differentia is the cytoskeleton of the cell. Endothelial cell has vimentin filaments while an epithelial cell has keratin filaments. [Onard].</rdfs:comment>\n19622:        <owl:annotatedTarget>An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal. In mammals, endothelial cell has vimentin filaments and is derived from the mesoderm.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n26091:                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n26110:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multi-ciliated epithelial cell located in the trachea and bronchi, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface. These cilia facilitate mucociliary clearance by moving mucus and trapped particles toward the pharynx.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n26115:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>ciliated columnar cell of tracheobronchial tree</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n26118:        <rdfs:comment>The marker set C1orf194, MS4A8 can identify the Human cell type ciliated columnar cell of tracheobronchial tree in the Lung with a confidence of 0.86 (NS-Forest FBeta value).</rdfs:comment>\n26120:        <rdfs:label>multiciliated columnar cell of tracheobronchial tree</rdfs:label>\n26125:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A multi-ciliated epithelial cell located in the trachea and bronchi, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface. These cilia facilitate mucociliary clearance by moving mucus and trapped particles toward the pharynx.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n26132:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The marker set C1orf194, MS4A8 can identify the Human cell type ciliated columnar cell of tracheobronchial tree in the Lung with a confidence of 0.86 (NS-Forest FBeta value).</owl:annotatedTarget>\n26279:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An epithelial cell present in the trachea and bronchi; columnar in shape; generally lack cilia; immature forms of ciliated or secretory cells which have been formed from stem cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n26283:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>undifferentiated columnar cell of tracheobronchial tree</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n26289:        <owl:annotatedTarget>An epithelial cell present in the trachea and bronchi; columnar in shape; generally lack cilia; immature forms of ciliated or secretory cells which have been formed from stem cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n26346:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cell of the cuboidal epithelium that covers the lens. The cells of the lens epithelium regulate most of the homeostatic functions of the lens. As ions, nutrients, and liquid enter the lens from the aqueous humor, Na+/K+ ATPase pumps in the lens epithelial cells pump ions out of the lens to maintain appropriate lens osmolarity and volume, with equatorially positioned lens epithelium cells contributing most to this current. The activity of the Na+/K+ ATPases keeps water and current flowing through the lens from the poles and exiting through the equatorial regions. The cells of the lens epithelium also serve as the progenitors for new lens fibers. It constantly lays down fibers in the embryo, fetus, infant, and adult, and continues to lay down fibers for lifelong growth.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n26357:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A cell of the cuboidal epithelium that covers the lens. The cells of the lens epithelium regulate most of the homeostatic functions of the lens. As ions, nutrients, and liquid enter the lens from the aqueous humor, Na+/K+ ATPase pumps in the lens epithelial cells pump ions out of the lens to maintain appropriate lens osmolarity and volume, with equatorially positioned lens epithelium cells contributing most to this current. The activity of the Na+/K+ ATPases keeps water and current flowing through the lens from the poles and exiting through the equatorial regions. The cells of the lens epithelium also serve as the progenitors for new lens fibers. It constantly lays down fibers in the embryo, fetus, infant, and adult, and continues to lay down fibers for lifelong growth.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n26808:                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n26815:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multi-ciliated epithelial cell located in the bronchus epithelium, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n26826:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A multi-ciliated epithelial cell located in the bronchus epithelium, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n28669:                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n28685:                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n28688:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell with multiple motile cilia on its apical surface. These cells facilitate the movement of liquids such as mucus or cerebrospinal fluid across the epithelial surface.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n28701:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell with multiple motile cilia on its apical surface. These cells facilitate the movement of liquids such as mucus or cerebrospinal fluid across the epithelial surface.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n30680:                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n33792:                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n33805:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multiciliated epithelial cell located in the respiratory tract epithelium, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface. This cell develops through a highly orchestrated process, transitioning from a basal progenitor via an intermediate deuterosomal cell stage that generates centrioles essential for ciliogenesis.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n33816:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A multiciliated epithelial cell located in the respiratory tract epithelium, characterized by a columnar shape and motile cilia on its apical surface. This cell develops through a highly orchestrated process, transitioning from a basal progenitor via an intermediate deuterosomal cell stage that generates centrioles essential for ciliogenesis.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n37652:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process in which the anatomical structures of the neural plate are generated and organized. The neural plate is a specialized region of columnar epithelial cells in the dorsal ectoderm that will give rise to nervous system tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n37660:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process in which the anatomical structures of the neural plate are generated and organized. The neural plate is a specialized region of columnar epithelial cells in the dorsal ectoderm that will give rise to nervous system tissue.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n37700:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of the neural plate over time, from its formation to the mature structure. The neural plate is a flat, thickened layer of ectodermal cells. The underlying dorsal mesoderm signals the ectodermal cells above it to elongate into columnar neural plate cells. The neural plate subsequently develops into the neural tube, which gives rise to the central nervous system.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n37708:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of the neural plate over time, from its formation to the mature structure. The neural plate is a flat, thickened layer of ectodermal cells. The underlying dorsal mesoderm signals the ectodermal cells above it to elongate into columnar neural plate cells. The neural plate subsequently develops into the neural tube, which gives rise to the central nervous system.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38615:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process in which a relatively unspecialized cell acquires specialized features of a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38618:        <rdfs:label>columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell differentiation</rdfs:label>\n38623:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process in which a relatively unspecialized cell acquires specialized features of a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38656:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell over time, from its formation to the mature structure. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38659:        <rdfs:label>columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell development</rdfs:label>\n38664:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell over time, from its formation to the mature structure. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38697:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process in which a relatively unspecialized cell acquires specialized features of a glandular epithelial cell. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38705:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process in which a relatively unspecialized cell acquires specialized features of a glandular epithelial cell. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38744:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of a glandular epithelial cell over time, from its formation to the mature structure. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38752:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of a glandular epithelial cell over time, from its formation to the mature structure. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38785:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The developmental process, independent of morphogenetic (shape) change, that is required for a columna/cuboidal epithelial cell to attain its fully functional state. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38788:        <rdfs:label>columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell maturation</rdfs:label>\n38793:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The developmental process, independent of morphogenetic (shape) change, that is required for a columna/cuboidal epithelial cell to attain its fully functional state. A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n38879:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The developmental process, independent of morphogenetic (shape) change, that is required for a glandular epithelial cell to attain its fully functional state. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n38887:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The developmental process, independent of morphogenetic (shape) change, that is required for a glandular epithelial cell to attain its fully functional state. A glandular epithelial cell is a columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface exposed to the lumen of a gland.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n71609:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The formation of the flat, thickened layer of ectodermal cells known as the neural plate. The underlying dorsal mesoderm signals the ectodermal cells above it to elongate into columnar neural plate cells. The neural plate subsequently develops into the neural tube, which gives rise to the central nervous system.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n71617:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The formation of the flat, thickened layer of ectodermal cells known as the neural plate. The underlying dorsal mesoderm signals the ectodermal cells above it to elongate into columnar neural plate cells. The neural plate subsequently develops into the neural tube, which gives rise to the central nervous system.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n131080:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any apoptotic process in an endothelial cell. An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n131096:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Any apoptotic process in an endothelial cell. An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n201335:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n227216:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>cuboidal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n231105:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n231126:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n231362:        <rdfs:label>columnar</rdfs:label>\n235673:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n235714:    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254 -->\n235716:    <owl:Class rdf:about=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\">\n235729:        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n242657:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n242678:        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254\"/>\n242989:    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007 -->\n242991:    <owl:Class rdf:about=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\">\n243001:        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource=\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0010007\"/>\n259014:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Portion of tissue, that consists of one or more layers of epithelial cells connected to each other by cell junctions and which is underlain by a basal lamina. Examples: simple squamous epithelium, glandular cuboidal epithelium, transitional epithelium, myoepithelium[CARO].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n259049:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Portion of tissue, that consists of one or more layers of epithelial cells connected to each other by cell junctions and which is underlain by a basal lamina. Examples: simple squamous epithelium, glandular cuboidal epithelium, transitional epithelium, myoepithelium[CARO].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n259067:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Unilaminar epithelium that consists of a single layer of cuboidal cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n259077:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Simple_cuboidal_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n259085:        <rdfs:label>simple cuboidal epithelium</rdfs:label>\n259090:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Unilaminar epithelium that consists of a single layer of cuboidal cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n259091:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Simple_cuboidal_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n259102:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Unilaminar epithelium, which consists of a single layer of columnar cells. Examples: ciliated columnar epithelium, gastric epithelium, microvillus columnar epithelium.[FMA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n259103:        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>columnar epithelium</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>\n259104:        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>columnar epithlium</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>\n259115:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Simple_columnar_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n259118:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>epithelium simplex columnare</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n259119:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>simple columnar epithelium</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n259124:        <rdfs:label>simple columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n259129:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Unilaminar epithelium, which consists of a single layer of columnar cells. Examples: ciliated columnar epithelium, gastric epithelium, microvillus columnar epithelium.[FMA].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n259131:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Simple_columnar_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n259137:        <owl:annotatedTarget>columnar epithelium</owl:annotatedTarget>\n259143:        <owl:annotatedTarget>columnar epithlium</owl:annotatedTarget>\n259153:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Epithelium which consists of more than one layer of epithelial cells that may or may not be in contact with a basement membrane. Examples: keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, ciliated stratified columnar epithelium.[FMA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n259179:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Epithelium which consists of more than one layer of epithelial cells that may or may not be in contact with a basement membrane. Examples: keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, ciliated stratified columnar epithelium.[FMA].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n272296:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mucous lining of the larynx, which is composed of squamous epithelium in the upper larynx and ciliated columnar epithelium in the lower larynx.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n272320:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The mucous lining of the larynx, which is composed of squamous epithelium in the upper larynx and ciliated columnar epithelium in the lower larynx.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n284792:        <obo:IAO_0000115>In amniote animal embryology, the epiblast is a tissue type derived either from the inner cell mass in mammals or the blastodisc in birds and reptiles. It lies above the hypoblast. In mammalian embryogenesis, the columnar cells of the epiblast are adjacent to the trophoblast, while the cuboidal cells of the hypoblast are closer to the blastocoele. The epiblast, whilst referred to as the primary ectoderm, differentiates to form all three layers of the trilaminar germ disc in a process called gastrulation[WP]. The outer of the two layers of the blastoderm that form during gastrulation, corresponding to primitive ectoderm during gastrulation and to the definitive ectoderm after gastrulation[ZFA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n284814:        <owl:annotatedTarget>In amniote animal embryology, the epiblast is a tissue type derived either from the inner cell mass in mammals or the blastodisc in birds and reptiles. It lies above the hypoblast. In mammalian embryogenesis, the columnar cells of the epiblast are adjacent to the trophoblast, while the cuboidal cells of the hypoblast are closer to the blastocoele. The epiblast, whilst referred to as the primary ectoderm, differentiates to form all three layers of the trilaminar germ disc in a process called gastrulation[WP]. The outer of the two layers of the blastoderm that form during gastrulation, corresponding to primitive ectoderm during gastrulation and to the definitive ectoderm after gastrulation[ZFA].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n316105:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A layer of cuboidal epithelial cells bounded anteriorly by the capsule of the lens and posteriorly by the lens cortex.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n316128:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A layer of cuboidal epithelial cells bounded anteriorly by the capsule of the lens and posteriorly by the lens cortex.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n322139:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Simple columnar epithelium that constitutes the secretory part of a gland. Examples: epithelium of stomach, luminal epithelium of lactiferous duct.[FMA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n322144:        <rdfs:label>glandular columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n322149:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Simple columnar epithelium that constitutes the secretory part of a gland. Examples: epithelium of stomach, luminal epithelium of lactiferous duct.[FMA].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n322174:        <rdfs:label>glandular cuboidal epithelium</rdfs:label>\n322250:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Simple columnar epithelium made up of cells specialized to serve as sensory cells for the reception of external stimuli, as the sensory cells of the cochlea, vestibule, nasal mucosa, and tongue.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n322261:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Simple columnar epithelium made up of cells specialized to serve as sensory cells for the reception of external stimuli, as the sensory cells of the cochlea, vestibule, nasal mucosa, and tongue.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n323511:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The structure from the trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles that forms the airways that supply air to the lungs. The lining of the tracheobronchial tree consists of ciliated columnar epithelial cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n323528:        <owl:annotatedTarget>The structure from the trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles that forms the airways that supply air to the lungs. The lining of the tracheobronchial tree consists of ciliated columnar epithelial cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n325418:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Simple columnar epithelium in which the luminal side of the cells bears cilia. Examples: epithelium of trachea, epithelium of uterine tube.[FMA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n325422:        <rdfs:label>ciliated columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n325427:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Simple columnar epithelium in which the luminal side of the cells bears cilia. Examples: epithelium of trachea, epithelium of uterine tube.[FMA].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n327446:        <rdfs:comment>The epiblast cells (appearing on day 8 of human embryonic development) make up a columnar epithelium with dense microvilli on the apical surface. During gastrulation, the epiblast cells undergo epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition and delaminate to become the loose mesenchyme of the primitive streak. The epiblast is present in postimplantation mouse embryos between E5.5-E7.5.</rdfs:comment>\n331626:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An epithelium consisting of cuboidal epithelial cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n331631:        <rdfs:label>cuboidal epithelium</rdfs:label>\n331636:        <owl:annotatedTarget>An epithelium consisting of cuboidal epithelial cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n334279:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A simple columnar epithelium that looks stratified but is not, because its cells are arranged with their nuclei at different levels.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n334283:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Pseudostratified_columnar_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n334286:        <rdfs:label>pseudostratified columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n334291:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A simple columnar epithelium that looks stratified but is not, because its cells are arranged with their nuclei at different levels.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n334292:        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>Wikipedia:Pseudostratified_columnar_epithelium</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>\n334317:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>epithelium pseudostratificatum columnare ciliatum (trachea et bronchi)</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\n334320:        <rdfs:label>pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n334331:        <owl:annotatedTarget>epithelium pseudostratificatum columnare ciliatum (trachea et bronchi)</owl:annotatedTarget>\n337887:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An epithelium that consists of columnar epithelial cells. Columnar epithelia are epithelial cells whose heights are at least four times their width. Columnar epithelia are divided into simple (or unilayered), and the rarer stratified (or multi-layered).[WP, modified].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n337891:        <rdfs:label>columnar epithelium</rdfs:label>\n337896:        <owl:annotatedTarget>An epithelium that consists of columnar epithelial cells. Columnar epithelia are epithelial cells whose heights are at least four times their width. Columnar epithelia are divided into simple (or unilayered), and the rarer stratified (or multi-layered).[WP, modified].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
